      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Transboundary pollution including airborne fine particulate matter (PM(2.5)) has been attracting attention in recent years and its health effects are concerned. We evaluated the short-term effects of air pollutants on pulmonary function among young students."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) and peak expiratory flow (PEF) were examined every morning for 1 month in 37 students who attend a school in a remote island of the Seto Inland Sea. The concentrations of fine and coarse particles (PM(2.5) and PM(10-2.5)), optical black carbon (OBC) and nitrogen dioxide (NO\u2082) were measured on the rooftop of the school."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The change in PEF was significantly associated with an increase in the prior 24-h average concentration of PM(2.5) among subjects with a history of asthma (-42.5 L/min [95% confidence interval (CI): -77.0, -8.1], for an interquartile rage increase of 17.6 μg/m³). Negative associations between pulmonary function and the concentrations of NO\u2082and OBC were observed as well. On the other hand, among the subjects with a history of allergy other than asthma and those without such history, negative association between air pollutants and pulmonary function was not observed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Subjects with a history of asthma are considered to be more affected by short-term exposure to air pollutants."
        }
      ]},
